          'A common mistake is not specifying the applicable standard and glass type clearly. Glass that does not meet the required standard or application can fail in service, causing safety issues and liability. Buyers should specify the exact standard, glass type (float, tempered, laminated), thickness and properties.',
          'Insufficient attention to packaging quality is the most common cause of breakage during transit. Glass must be properly packed in crates with protective materials between sheets, secured to prevent movement. Inadequate packaging leads to scratching, edge chipping and breakage. Buyers should verify packaging standards and request photos of packaged goods.',
          'Not verifying building code compliance for safety glass is a regulatory risk. Safety glass (tempered, laminated) used in buildings must meet national safety standards. Non-compliant glass can fail inspection and require replacement. Buyers should verify compliance requirements and request certification.',
          'Overlooking thermal performance for energy-efficient buildings is a specification issue. For energy-efficient buildings, the glass U-value, solar heat gain coefficient and visible light transmission should be specified to meet the building\'s energy performance requirements. Standard clear float glass may not meet energy code requirements.',
